Tab 5 – Summit P&L
"Let's now move to the Summit P&L tab.
This tab represents the Summit side of the reconciliation and serves as our reference dataset for comparing valuations and performance measures against Murex.
The data is obtained directly from Summit through a SQL query executed in the performance database. The query is run for a specific valuation date and returns the key measures that we need for reconciliation.
For each position, the extract contains:

Day-to-Day (DTD) P&L,
Market Value,
Accrued Interest,
Outstanding Notional.

These are some of the most important metrics used in the validation process because they allow us to compare both the valuation and performance calculations produced by the two systems.
The purpose of this tab is not only to identify differences, but also to explain them.
As I mentioned earlier, Market Value is generally driven by several underlying components, including price, accrued interest, and notional. When we observe a difference between Summit and Murex, we need to determine whether the discrepancy originates from:

The position or outstanding notional,
The accrued interest calculation,
The security price,
Currency conversion,
Or another valuation methodology difference.

This Summit extract provides the reference values against which the Murex results are compared.
Because the data is extracted directly from Summit for a given date, it represents the official production-side valuation information that we use during the UAT2 validation process.
In summary, this tab contains the key valuation and performance measures from Summit and forms the foundation of the comparison against the corresponding Murex data. Together with the Murex Subaccount P&L and Bond Position extracts, it enables us to perform a detailed reconciliation and root-cause analysis of any differences identified."